    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Post-traumatic osteoarthritis of the left hindfoot (M19.172)</content>. Pain 4 of 10 as the injection wears off; function still much better than at intake. A second injection can be given after three months from the first, in two weeks or later, if he wishes; surgery remains his choice to raise. [S]<list><item>A second ultrasound-guided subtalar injection booked in three weeks at his request.</item><item>Continue the brace, home exercises, diclofenac gel and acetaminophen as needed.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Prediabetes (R73.03)</content>. Hemoglobin A1c 5.8%, from 6.0%, with weight down 2 kg. [S]<list><item>Continue the prevention program; hemoglobin A1c yearly.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Food insecurity (Z59.41)</content>. Enough food through the month with the food distribution program and the garden. [S]<list><item>The Community Health Representative will check in before winter.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Pain on walking (0 to 10 numeric rating scale)</td><td>Every visit and after each treatment trial</td><td>Completed today</td><td>4 of 10, eleven weeks after the injection</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Function: Foot and Ankle Ability Measure, activities of daily living subscale</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>76%, from 81% at ten weeks and 52% at baseline</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Joint examination: hindfoot and ankle motion, alignment, swelling and gait</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Painful end-range subtalar motion; no swelling</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Exercise, physical therapy and brace use</td><td>Every visit; physical therapy weekly for six weeks</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Home exercises and the walking group</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>NSAID safety: blood pressure, creatinine and blood count; stomach symptoms</td><td>Before an oral NSAID, then every 6 to 12 months on long-term use</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>No oral NSAID since the sick visit</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Imaging</td><td>At diagnosis or for surgical planning; not for routine follow-up</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>No new symptoms; no surgery planned</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Blood lead with retained bullet fragments</td><td>Once when fragments are found; repeat only if fragments lie in a joint or bone, or symptoms suggest lead toxicity</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>No symptoms of lead toxicity; fragments outside the joints</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Blood pressure, with home readings to confirm or exclude hypertension</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>122/76 mmHg</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Depression screening, sleep and coping</td><td>At baseline and periodically</td><td>Completed today</td><td>PHQ-9 3, item 9 scored 0</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Hemoglobin A1c in prediabetes</td><td>At diagnosis, then in three to four months and yearly</td><td>Completed today</td><td>5.8%, from 6.0%</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Food access and the food distribution program</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Food distribution program and a larger garden; enough food through the month</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Traditional healing alongside clinic care, at his choice</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>His own ways of healing continue, as he chooses; nothing asked</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Walk a mile on uneven ground and ride with pain of 3 or less. Outcome by 2026-09-14: Pain 7 to 3 of 10 on walking; walking more than a mile with the brace; riding again [S]</item><item>Decide about surgery with full information, in his own time. Outcome by 2026-09-14: Arthrodesis explained with its risks and recovery; he chose to wait and will raise it again if needed [S]</item><item>Bring hemoglobin A1c below 5.7%. Outcome by 2026-10-26: 6.0 to 5.8%, with more walking, less flour, lard and sugar, and more garden vegetables and bison [S]</item><item>Enough food through the month. Outcome by 2026-10-26: Enrolled in the Food Distribution Program on Indian Reservations, including bison and other traditional foods, and a larger garden [S]</item></list><paragraph>Immunizations given today: Tdap vaccine, deferred from the preventive visit at his request; Hepatitis B vaccine (HepB-CpG), first of two doses; screening showed he is not immune [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Guideline basis: 2019 American College of Rheumatology/Arthritis Foundation Guideline for the Management of Osteoarthritis of the Hand, Hip, and Knee (2019); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, rheumatoid arthritis and osteoarthritis and depression, bipolar, and mood disorders monographs (2026); Indian Health Manual, Part 3, Chapter 30: Chronic Non-Cancer Pain Management (2026); Special General Memorandum 94-08: Statement of Policy for the Traditional Cultural Advocacy Program (1994); Special Diabetes Program for Indians (2026); Food Distribution Program on Indian Reservations (2026); Prediabetes and Type 2 Diabetes: Screening (2021); Adult Immunization Schedule by Age (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Tdap and the first hepatitis B dose given today; second hepatitis B dose in one month or more; second zoster dose in two to six months after the first. Subtalar injection in three weeks. Review in three months. [S]</paragraph></text>

